const content="<article data-editorial-version=\"3\" class=\"sn-page\"><div class=\"sn-read\"><a href=\"/supporters/\">← Featured sponsors</a><p class=\"sn-kicker\">Marine Diesel Engine · Sponsor feature</p><img class=\"sn-logo\" src=\"/images/supporters/sos-marine/feed-logo.webp\" alt=\"SOS Marine logo\" width=\"210\" height=\"100\"><h1>SOS Marine: rescue equipment for getting someone back aboard</h1><p class=\"sn-intro\">SOS Marine makes lifejackets, rafts, man-overboard markers and recovery equipment to help protect people and bring them back aboard boats. For owners, the range addresses the people aboard: helping support someone in the water, locate them and assist with returning them to the boat.</p><img class=\"sn-hero\" src=\"/images/supporters/sos-marine/dan-buoy-detail.webp\" alt=\"SOS Marine Dan Buoy product photograph\" width=\"1158\" height=\"800\"><section><h2>Recovery needs its own equipment choices</h2><p>Once a boat reaches a person, getting them over the side can still be difficult. SOS Marine offers recovery ladders and Sea Scoopa, alongside the SOS Dan Buoy marker and flotation equipment.</p><p>We like that the product range distinguishes those jobs. Flotation, marking and recovery are separate tasks, and each can be assessed around the boat&#x27;s layout and the crew available to help.</p></section><section><h2>A specialist Australian maker</h2><p>The company manufactures in Botany, Sydney, for leisure, professional and defence applications. Its focus gives owners specific marine safety products to investigate rather than a general assortment of accessories.</p><p>For us, the value is in being able to connect the equipment with an actual requirement. A boat&#x27;s high sides or a small crew can make recovery a practical challenge that deserves thought before departure.</p></section><section><h2>Why we feature SOS Marine</h2><p>Owners invest time in keeping engines ready because dependable operation matters. The equipment carried for crew and guests deserves deliberate attention alongside the machinery.</p><p>We feature SOS Marine for its direct safety and rescue purpose. The range includes products for several stages of helping someone in the water, with particular attention to the physical task of bringing them aboard. That is useful equipment to understand while there is time to plan for the vessel and its people.</p></section><div class=\"sn-contact\"><a href=\"https://www.sosmarine.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Discover SOS Marine ↗</a><a href=\"/supporters/\">More featured sponsors</a></div></div></article><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"Article\", \"headline\": \"SOS Marine: rescue equipment for getting someone back aboard\", \"description\": \"SOS Marine makes lifejackets, rafts, man-overboard markers and recovery equipment to help protect people and bring them back aboard boats.\", \"image\": \"https://marinedieselengine.com.au/images/supporters/sos-marine/dan-buoy-detail.webp\", \"author\": {\"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Marine Diesel Engine\"}, \"dateModified\": \"2026-11-05\", \"mainEntityOfPage\": \"https://marinedieselengine.com.au/supporters/sos-marine/\"}</script>";
